{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261030T132441Z-95fcadca", "ts": "2026-10-30T13:29:00Z", "actor": {"worktree": "arrt", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "feature/delete-a-wall", "build": "0d265237c4dd"}, "body": {"base_commit": "3ba2812fcddab19daeee26b7f72c5da173102876", "base_tree": "12bef23b75786da1555bb0242882a5aa95dcaf51", "head_tree": "4a56d3b964267870fc439a9687129930ef75dd07", "head_commit": "cff2d4eb6dd69072e9162c8e49d79cf349928d7a", "dispatch_commit": "cff2d4eb6dd69072e9162c8e49d79cf349928d7a", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/api.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/status.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/bindings.py", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/tools.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/sqlite.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/store.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_displays_and_labels.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_scenes_on_walls.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_sidebar.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_walls.py", "arrt/tests/contract/test_player_surface.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_deleting_a_wall_surfaces.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_deleting_a_wall.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-delete-a-wall.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/information-architecture.md", ".prawduct/change-log.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", "arrt/src/arrt/http/api.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/status.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/bindings.py", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/tools.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/sqlite.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/store.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_displays_and_labels.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_scenes_on_walls.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_sidebar.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_walls.py", "arrt/tests/contract/test_player_surface.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_deleting_a_wall_surfaces.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_deleting_a_wall.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-delete-a-wall.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/information-architecture.md", ".prawduct/change-log.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "file_had_table crashes catalogue open when ART_ROOT is relative", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `migrations.file_had_table` builds its read-only URI with `Path(path).as_uri()`, and `as_uri()` raises ValueError ('relative paths can't be expressed as file URIs') for a relative path. `Settings.from_env` takes `Path(ART_ROOT)` unresolved (config.py:576), and neither config nor `art_root.prepare` requires it to be absolute. So a deployment or dev run with a relative ART_ROOT and an existing catalogue fails to start: the server, `arrt.seed` and `arrt.identify` all go through `open_catalogue_file`, which opened that file fine before this branch. Every test passes `tmp_path`, which is absolute, so the suite cannot see it. Fix: use `Path(path).resolve().as_uri()` (or `.absolute()`), and add a unit test that opens a catalogue by a relative path (monkeypatch.chdir into tmp_path) with a `walls` table already present, so it reaches the URI line. Watch the test fail on the current code first.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py", "arrt/src/arrt/persistence/file.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Plan Done-when 'the axe scan covers the dialog and the empty page' has no evidence and no recorded descope", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Chunk 03's Done-when and the plan's accessibility-spec disposition (norm 2: 'the axe scan covers the Edit\u2026 dialog with *Delete\u2026* in it and the empty Walls page') both promise an axe scan. The browser suite runs no axe. The scan is `arrt/tools/ux_walk.py`, run by hand, and it scans pages: it throws away clicks that open a dialog without navigating, and it cannot reach the empty Walls page without a catalogue that has no walls. The delete-a-wall change-log entry names no scan, while earlier entries record 'The axe scan \u2026 is clean' when one was run. Either run the scan on the Edit\u2026 dialog with Delete\u2026 and on the no-wall page and record what it found, or record the descope in the plan and the change-log. Don't leave the conformance claim standing without evidence.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "No test holds that a deleted wall's showings are kept", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Chunk 02's Done-when asks for 'A unit test per row of the table, each with a wall that has that dependant and a second wall that keeps its own'. The `showings` row ('kept, ruling 1') has no test, and `test_its_history_stays_and_names_it` only checks history events, with no second-wall assertion. A later cascade that deleted showings with the wall (for example, someone 'tidying up' `delete_wall`, or a foreign key added to `showings`) would pass the suite and break ruling 1. Add a test that records a showing on both walls, deletes one, and asserts both walls' showings are still there (for example via `latest_showing`).", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_deleting_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Delete\u2026's confirmation branches for a hung selection, an unnamed Player and several panels are untested", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `deletingSays` has six worded outcomes. The browser tests reach two: everything present (a named theme, a named Player, one panel) and nothing present (Cancel). Three are never asserted: 'It hangs a selection, which goes once no wall hangs it.', the fallback 'Its Player stops showing it.', and 'Its N label panels stop captioning.'. The selection sentence is the one that tells the curator something will be destroyed. A swapped condition would tell them a selection 'stays in your themes' when the delete retires it. Add one browser case with a hung selection and two label panels, and assert the exact consequence text. Per the learnings rule 'When a clause or notice is conditional, assert its absence in the case where it must not appear', also assert that the theme sentence is absent in that case.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "A live scene released after its wall is deleted writes a nameless History line and a spurious 'no feed' warning", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The live scene keeps the deleted wall's pin (ruling 2), so `release_scene` or a replacing `apply_scene` goes through `_record_scene`. `_record_scene` writes a `scene.ended` event with `wall_id` set to the deleted wall and no `wall_name`, because `get_wall` returns None. That contradicts data-model.md's new sentence that every kind naming a wall carries `wall_name` from 2026-10-30. `_patch_walls` also logs 'Wall \u2026 has no feed, so its part in a scene is not published.' at WARNING for a wall that was deleted on purpose. `test_a_live_scene_goes_on\u2026` releases the scene but checks neither. Cheapest fix: skip pins whose wall is no longer recorded in `_record_scene` and `_patch_walls`, as `scene_walls` already does. Alternatively, record that this line is nameless.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Interrupted first open of a pre-walls file now drops which theme was hung", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `SqliteDurableStore` commits the schema (durable.py:234) before it runs migrations. A pre-walls file interrupted between those two steps reopens with an empty `walls` table and `themes.is_active` still present. `seed` is now False, so `establish_the_wall` goes straight to `_drop_what_the_wall_replaced`. That drops `is_active` without carrying the hung theme, and the file is left with no wall. Before this branch the next open seeded and carried. The docstring accepts this edge only for a *new* file. One line closes it: seed when `seed or _has_column(connection, 'themes', 'is_active')`. Very few files are affected (pre-2026-08-12 files, during a crash window), so this is a note.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A live scene's pin to a deleted wall is a dangling reference each reader tolerates differently", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Cause: delete_wall deliberately keeps the live scene's pin (the owner's ruling), but nothing models 'a pin whose wall is gone'. Each reader handles the missing row on its own terms. scene_walls skips it. _wall_names prints the bare id. _record_scene writes scene.ended without wall_name. _patch_walls, called by release_scene and apply/replace, finds no feed and logs WARNING 'Wall <id> has no feed, so its part in a scene is not published', a branch written for 'a feed removed since', so every release after such a delete puts a false fault in the journal for an operator to chase. The same gap makes the new data-model.md sentence 'Every kind that names a wall carries wall_name from 2026-10-30' untrue for a scene.ended written after the wall's delete. Fix with one owner: a single helper (e.g. `_recorded_pins(scene)`) that the end/patch/record paths take pins through. It should skip pins whose wall is not recorded, or carry the name forward from the scene.applied detail. Then pin it with a test that releases a live scene after one of its walls is deleted and asserts no warning and a named history line. Search every `list_pins` / `view.pins` reader in programming/display.py, not only the sites listed here.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "establish_the_wall has two branches that do the same thing", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The new `if not seed:` branch repeats the `if _walls(connection):` branch above it word for word: drop what the wall replaced, return. Merge them into `if _walls(connection) or not seed:` so a later change to the early-return path cannot land in only one of them. Optional; nothing is wrong today.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "art_display's `deleted` key carries two shapes across its delete actions", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `delete_wall` answers `deleted={wall_id, name}`, while the existing `delete_theme` binding answers `deleted=<theme_id>` (a bare string). Both are documented in api-contract.md, so nothing is broken. But an agent that learned `deleted` from one action will misread it on the other. Either is acceptable to keep. If a third delete action is added, settle one shape for the surface rather than adding a third.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/mcp/bindings.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Decisions in the delete-a-wall bundle, and what settled each", "recommendation": "Scope: none, for the owner. Settled by the owner's four rulings (build-plan \u00a7 Goals): permanent with history kept; the live-scene pin kept and the scene goes on; a hung theme does not stop the delete; the last wall may go. Settled in planning and inferred from those rulings: seed only when the file never had a `walls` table, with no stored marker; no new event kind, with `wall_name` added to `work.left_theme` and `work.excluded`; the live pin kept as the scene's record. Carried from the #400 review: the press guard keyed per dialog rather than per button (`readingOnce`), and a late read opening nothing (`control.isConnected`). Made mid-build, recorded only in the change log or a docstring: five `art_display` descriptions shortened to fit the 2 KB menu budget (change-log); a new file interrupted between its schema and the seed opens with no wall, accepted (`establish_the_wall` docstring only, not data-model.md); `work.excluded` carries `wall_name` only when a wall was named; three existing browser tests re-counted for the Delete panel (change-log). Alignment-pass miss: the accepted interrupted-file edge contradicts data-model.md's 'an empty walls table is one whose curator deleted them all'. See the learnings cross-check note below.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/tools.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "A catalogue that opens with no wall, and so is not seeded, logs nothing", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The new `if not seed:` branch in `establish_the_wall` returns silently. Two causes lead there: the curator deleted every wall, or (the accepted edge) a new file was interrupted before its seed ran. The seeding path logs 'Established wall \u2026'. The no-seed path leaves an operator looking at an empty Walls page with nothing in the journal to say why no wall came back. The health reading's 'No wall is recorded' does make the state detectable, so this is a NOTE. If it is worth one line: add `log.info` when the walls table exists, is empty and is deliberately left unseeded.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: two 'every'/'is one whose' claims about walls that the code does not enforce", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (two sites). Rule cited: 'When you write or cite 'exactly one', 'always' or 'never', state what the store or guard actually enforces'. (1) data-model.md now says 'Every kind that names a wall carries `wall_name` from 2026-10-30'. But `_record_scene` adds `wall_name` only while `get_wall` finds the wall. Ruling 2 keeps a deleted wall's pin in the live scene, so the `scene.ended` event later written for that pin carries `wall_id` without a name. Activity then renders 'a wall no longer recorded' for a deletion made after this change, which is the exact case the planning decision ('a deleted wall's history still names it going forward') set out to cover. Either carry the name for a kept pin, or make the claim relational ('every kind written while its wall is recorded'). (2) data-model.md says 'A file whose `walls` table is empty is one whose curator deleted them all', and the comment in `status.js` ('none means every wall was deleted') says the same. The `establish_the_wall` docstring accepts a second cause, a new file interrupted before its seed. Make both say 'usually', or name the edge. Neither clears a Records Pass bar, because no operator acts wrongly on them. Other surface rules checked and found satisfied: 'Before a UI or notice says 'do X to fix this', grep for the endpoint and control' (the empty page's *Add wall\u2026* exists and is tested) and 'Before advertising an action, build a real call using only shipped surfaces' (`delete_wall`'s `wall_id` comes from `walls`).", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/status.js"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/arrt#391 (programming: a wall cannot be deleted)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update 391 status=shipped closed-by=scope:delete-a-wall` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. The change log already says 'Closes #391'.", "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/arrt#400 (curation-ui: a wall's Edit\u2026 opened before a repaint shows stale state)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update 400 status=shipped closed-by=scope:walls-stale-edit` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. This is the only item `affecting` returns for the changed files.", "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "A recurring browser flake is recorded only in the change log, with no backlog item", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The change log records that `test_asking.py::test_a_returning_page_draws_the_thread_as_it_was` passed only on retry. The archive (2026-10.md, around line 1017) shows the same test flaking one run in three before a fix on 2026-10-09, so this is a fix that has regressed, not a first sighting. The repo files flakes as items (#397, #57), but the backlog cache has none for this test. A line in an append-only change log is not where the next session looks. File a `tests:` item that cites both sightings.", "fid": "R-15"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 10}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "delete-a-wall", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "3ba2812fcddab19daeee